hello everyone and welcome to today's  webinar changes to usp  643 total organic carbon from  pharmaceutical  grade waters i'm susie valdez of lab  roots and i will be your moderator for  today's  event today's educational web seminar is  presented by  lab roots and brought to you by beckmann  coulter life sciences  to learn more visit beckman.com  now we encourage you to participate  today by submitting as many questions as  you have during the presentation  to do so simply type them into the ask a  question box and click send  we'll answer as many questions as we  have time for at the end  of the presentation you may also submit  any technical issues here as well  if you have any trouble seeing or  hearing the presentation  i'd now like to welcome our speaker tony  harrison  a senior marketing manager at beckmann  coulter life  sciences tony welcome you may now begin  your presentation  thank you very much for that  introduction i appreciate it and welcome  to everybody  today's short presentation on on the new  chapter  for the usb 643 on total organic carbon  uh my name's tony harrison i am a senior  manager at beckman quarter life sciences  so a revised version of the usb chapter  on toc  total organic carbon the usp 643 chapter  it's been published already uh but it  will become official on the 1st of may  uh this year 2021. this presentation  gives an overview  of this new document and discusses the  difference also  uh in the system suitability test  materials specified  in usb 643 and those specified in the  japanese  pharmacopoeia for toc  now total organic carbon is one of the  four  critical quality parameters defined by  the farmer computers for bulk purified  water  water injection water hemodialysis and  pure  steam condensate plus of course packaged  sterile waters  the other three are conductivity  microbial colony forming units  and endotoxins  usp 643 points out that toc  is an indirect measurement of organic  contamination  and that wealth tfc can be a food for  microbes encouraging their proliferation  in water systems  there's no direct numerical correlation  between toc levels in a water system  and microbial contamination levels  it goes on to state that toc can be  introduced to a bulk water system from  the source water  from the purification and distribution  systems  and from biofilm growing in the system  for packaged sterile water an additional  source of toc  can be the packaging itself  usb 643 states that toc is a limit  test and that means that the levels of  toc in a sample  must not breach the defined limit which  is the response of the toc analyzer to a  500 ppb standard  made up from usb traceable sucrose  so in other words whatever the toc  result the analyzer gives in response to  testing the usb  traceable 500 ppv carbon sucrose  becomes the maximum limit of toc  allowable in the water system  so the limit may not be a reported  result of 500 ppb of toc  and users should be aware of this and  ensure that the measured toc levels in  their water systems  does not exceed the limit reported by  their toc analyzer  when analyzing the 500 ppb carbon  usb traceable sucrose standard just  as an example if the recorded toc result  from the  toc analyzer in response to analyzing  500 ppv sucrose  is a to toc result of 450 ppp  then a reported toc result from a water  sample of 450 ppp  becomes the maximum allowable level of  toc for their water sample  not 500  now usp 643 does not mandate the  measurement technology it should be used  to measure toc  just that whatever is used should have a  manufacturer's specified limited  detection  of 0.05 milligrams per liter which is  equivalent to  0.05 ppm or 50 ppb  now that it should pass the test for  suitability  the system suitability test usp 643  states that the analyzer should meet  measure the toc by  oxidizing the organic molecules to  produce carbon dioxide  and then measure the carbon dioxide from  the toc to calculate the amount of toc  present in the water sample it does not  mandate  on or offline instruments but it goes on  to state that any technology used to  measure toc in water  must be capable of discriminating  between the toc  and the inorganic carbon maybe already  be present in the sample  total inorganic carbon it's called such  as dissolved  carbon dioxide that may be already  present in the sample or bicarbonate  now this discrimination can be achieved  either by purging the tic from the  sample before  oxidizing the organics or by measuring  the tic in the sample  and then subtracting that from the final  total organic carbon measurement  made after all the organics have been  oxidized to carbon  dioxide total carbon so  some analyzers measure tc and tic  through the mass and calculate  toc and this is perfectly acceptable  according to the usp  now when the toc test was introduced in  after the united states farm  here in 1998 it replaced several of the  tests  and the challenge was to ensure that  instruments used for trc analysis  would be suitable for analyzing the  range of organic compounds  expected to be found in pharmaceutical  grade waters  the system suitability test was  introduced as a method to verify  that a toc analyzer could analyze with  equal efficiency  a solution containing the relatively  easy to oxidize substance sucrose  as well as a solution containing the  longer chain and more difficult to  oxidize substance benzoquinone  both solutions must contain 500 ppp of  carbon  and a toc analyzer must analyze both 500  ppv solutions  equally well and produce toc results  from both  that when within plus or minus 15 of  each other  now i stated earlier the toc test is a  limit test  with a maximum allowable level in bulk  pharmaceutical grade waters  of 500 ppb of carbon now whatever  the toc result the analyzer gives in  response to testing that usb traceable  500 ppv  sucrose solution becomes the maximum  limit  of toc allowable in the water system so  the limit may not be 500 ppb  and as i said earlier users should be  aware of this  and ensure that the measured levels of  toc in their water  does not exceed the limit response  reported by their toc analyzer  when analyzing that 500 ppb sucrose  started  now this section here the system seems  to be suitability testing for packaged  sterile water  this is where the substantive changes to  usb 643 have occurred  in addition to passing the system  suitability test with the same plus or  minus 15  limit it now allows for different  concentrations of  toc depending on the container size for  the packaged water  and this is called l1 and as long as the  water sample being tested contains less  toc than  l1 it is deemed to have passed the test  and its  test is complete if however the reported  toc value from a water sample is higher  than  l1 but lower than the second defined  higher limit  l2 the user is required to determine  exactly  what organic compounds are present in  the water  using a suitable analytical procedure  if the quantity of every organic  compound present  measured individually is less than 0.2  milligrams per liter which is less or  equal to  0.2 ppm or less than equal to 200 ppp  then they are deemed not to be present  in a quantity that may be harmful to  patients safety  if however any individual organic  compound is found to be present in  quantities  greater than 0.2 milligrams per liter  then  that organic compound must be evaluated  for their impact on patient safety  at that reported concentration  if it's determined that there's no  impact on patient safety  then the water sample meets the  requirements and again the test is  complete  now if however any organic compounds are  identified as potentially harmful  and are present in sufficient  concentration to impact  patient state safety then the water  sample does not pass the test and fails  additionally if the toc analysis from  the water sample shows that the level  is higher than the second limit l2  then again the sample does not pass the  test and there's no requirement to  identify  and quantify individual organic organic  compounds  however of course the user may require  them to do this as part of their root  cause investigation but it's not  mandated  uh in islamic appeal  so this is the um the different package  uh the water container volumes that the  usb new usb  chapter uses less than equal to five mil  greater than five mil and less than  equal to 100 mil  and then greater than 100 mil and you  can see there's two limits  for toc l1 l2  and limit l1 is the first one as long as  it's below that  then the the past is a pass  if it's higher than l1 lower than l2  that's when you have to analyze  to determine what organics are present  and in what quantities  and are they harmful for patients if not  you pass great if however the test  company  comes with a result higher than l2  in the first place then it fails  and those maximum limits vary  according to the container size as you  can see  by the table and just the point of  interest the l2  limits are actually 50 percent higher if  you look at the table then  those are the l1 limits  so i did mention i'd comment on the  differences between the united states  pharmacopoeia  and the japanese pharmacopoeia on toc  now unlike usp 643 chapter  which is primarily aimed at  pharmaceutical waters  the japanese chapter on toc is a more  general chapter  and includes also reference to toc  analyzers  that are used for cleaning validation  too  and that's a fundamental difference so  as the organic compounds found in  cleaning rinse waters  may be more complex and difficult to  oxidize molecules  than those expected in pharmaceutical  waters the japanese  pharmacopoeia chapter on toc calls for  the toc analyzer to be challenged  with the solution of the ionic  surfactant  sdbs  so it's typical an ionic surfactant it  could be used in the cleaning process so  if they they expect you to be able to  analyze sdbs  now another difference is that the  japanese chapter gives  some guidance on calibration which is  not the case with usp643  the japanese chapter instructs the user  to use khp  as a calibration solution now khp is a  relatively easy to oxidize solution it's  very stable  and it's frequently used to calibrate  calibrate general purpose laboratory toc  analyzers  in contrast usp 643 does not specify how  a toc analyzer should be calibrated  nor what the calibration solution should  be made from but frequently  users will calibrate their toc analyzers  used to measure toc  levels in bulk waters water for  injection purified water  and they'll use calibration solutions  made up from sucrose because  that is the reference material specified  us by usb  643 to determine the toc analyzer's  maximum limit  for toc in both quarters  so this is bringing me to the end of my  uh presentation today  and in conclusion um i'd like to draw  parallels  between this new uh usp643 chapter  and the subvisible particle  contamination in parental drugs testing  chapter usp 788 where for small volume  containers the pass fail criteria  is based on the total number of  particles per container  and the change the test for packaged  sterile water and usb 643  is suggesting that the total amount of  organic carbon per container  is also a measure of patient safety not  just keeping toc levels between  below effect concentration as it would  be in bulk quarters  it is also asking users to do further  testing  when certain levels of toc are breached  to identify  each organic compound in the water to  better understand the risk to patients  now this is a significant change and it  will hopefully bring improved confidence  that sterile packaged water is safe for  human use  so thank you for intending uh sorry  attending today i'll now take  any questions that you have  and thank you tony for that informative  presentation  we will now start the live q a portion  of the webinar  if you have any questions you would like  to ask please do so now  just click on that ask a question box  located on the far  left of your screen and we'll answer as  many questions as we have time for  so let's take a look it looks like we  already have some  great questions coming in  our first question from an audience  member says thank you for your  presentation  please explain one more time why the  maximum  level of allowed for packaged  sterile waters is so much higher than  for  purified water and water for injection  great thank you very much for your  question and thanks for attending so  um just to go over that again the usp  chapter  acknowledges that whilst there are water  is derived from either purified water  or water injection it's most likely that  there will be  additional toc contamination from the  container now  that's either because it's a toc is  present in the container when it's  filled  or another way it gets in there is  because these waters tend to leach  ions from the plastic or glass container  because they're actually  very aggressive reagents and remember  sterile waters usually undergo some sort  of final sterilization process  step so the presence of toc or higher  levels of toc  is unlikely to encourage bacterial  growth because any bacteria  should be either dead or sterilized  thank you so much our next question  please  can you explain again why sucrose or  benzoquinone  were chosen for the system suitability  test  in usp 643  certainly certainly so this this often  confuses so the toc test as i mentioned  it was introduced going to the united  states pharmacy in 1998 and it  actually replaced several other tests  and as i mentioned the usp work  committee were concerned to ensure that  any toc instrument  used to perform these tests should be  capable of successfully oxidizing  the range of organic materials to be  found in purified water and water  injection  so they devise a system suitability test  which challenges the toc  instrument with a relatively short chain  easy to oxidize  sucrose solution and a longer chain more  difficult to oxidize benzoquinone  solution  now as long as the toc instrument can  oxidize both solutions  equally well then it's deemed to be  suitable for testing  toc in purified water and water for  injection  thank you so much tony and we have one  more question from our audience  why does the japanese pharmacopoeia call  for  system suitability testing on sdbs  only good question and as i said earlier  the the scope  for the japanese pharmacopoeia excuse me  is broader  than that of the usp chapter on toc and  the japanese one  encompasses not only toc testing for  purified water and water injection  but also cleaning validation and the jp  recognizes that laboratory toc analyzers  primarily  are frequently used for both purified  water water injection testing  and cleaning validation where first of  all much higher levels of toc  may be present and much more complex  organic molecules such as cleaning  solutions may be present  so in order to ensure the toc instrument  can handle both  applications the jp challenges the toc  instrument to ensure that it can  successfully  oxidize and analyze the complex and  difficult to oxidize  sdbs solution now as long as the  analyzer can successfully recover more  than 90  of the toc from the fpvs solution  then the analyzer is deemed to be  suitable for both purified water  and water for injection testing as well  as cleaning validation  tony i want to thank you again for your  time today and for your important  research and i also want to thank lab  roots and our sponsor beckman coulter  life sciences  for underwriting today's educational  webcast  and before we go i want to thank our  audience for joining us today and for  their interesting questions  questions we did not have time for today  and those submitted during the on-demand  period  will be addressed by the speaker via the  contact information you provided at the  time  of registration this webcast can be  viewed on demand  laborats will alert you via email when  it's available for replay  we encourage you to share that email  with your colleagues who may have missed  today's live event  until next time take care and stay  healthy bye bye everyone

